 To create a simple form that accepts and saves user-inputted text, I will use HTML for the structure, CSS for styling, and JavaScript for interactivity. Here's an example using plain HTML, CSS, and JavaScript:

HTML (index.html):
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <title>Form Example</title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">
</head>
<body>
  <form id="myForm">
    <label for="textInput">Enter your text:</label>
    <input type="text" id="textInput" name="textInput">
    <button type="submit">Save Text</button>
  </form>
  <h2>Saved Text:</h2>
  <div id="savedText"></div>
  <script src="app.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

CSS (styles.css):
```css
* {
  box-sizing: border-box;
}

body {
  font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
}

#myForm {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  width: 100%;
  height: 100vh;
}

label, input, button {
  margin: 5px;
}

#savedText {
  border: 1px solid #ccc;
  padding: 10px;
  width: 100%;
}
```

JavaScript (app.js):
```javascript
const form = document.getElementById('myForm');
const textInput = document.getElementById('textInput');
const savedText = document.getElementById('savedText');

form.addEventListener('submit', function(e) {
  e.preventDefault();

  const userText = textInput.value;

  if (userText) {
    saveText(userText);
    clearForm();
  }
});

function saveText(text) {
  // Save the text to local storage or an API, depending on your needs
  localStorage.setItem('savedText', text);
}

function clearForm() {
  textInput.value = '';
}

// Load saved text from local storage, if it exists
function loadSavedText() {
  const savedText = localStorage.getItem('savedText');

  if (savedText) {
    savedText.split('\n').forEach(function(line) {
      addLineToSavedText(line);
    });
  }
}

// Add a line of text to the savedText div
function addLineToSavedText(text) {
  const savedTextDiv = document.getElementById('savedText');
  savedTextDiv.innerHTML += `<p>${text}</p>`;
}

loadSavedText();
```

This example creates a simple form that saves user-inputted text to the local storage, and it also loads any previously saved texts when the page is loaded. The JavaScript code adds an event listener to the form's submit event, which saves the user's input when the form is submitted, and clears the input field afterward. Additionally, the script initializes the form with any previously saved text from local storage, if it exists.
